// audit-ci configuration for TaskBuddy CI (task A6).
//
// Runs `npm audit` and FAILS the build on any advisory at moderate severity or
// above that is NOT in the allowlist below. This turns `npm audit` into a
// meaningful gate (it catches NEW vulnerabilities) instead of permanent red
// noise from known dev/build-only advisories.
//
// Usage in CI (add to .github/workflows/ci.yml after the build step):
//   - name: Security audit (allowlisted)
//     run: npx --yes audit-ci@^7 --config ./audit-ci.jsonc
//
// The allowlist mirrors the "Accepted" dependency findings (DEP-02..DEP-06) in
// .do_not_upload/security_assessment.md. DEP-02..DEP-05 are dev/build/test only
// and not reachable in the running service. DEP-01 (nodemailer runtime advisory,
// GHSA-p6gq-j5cr-w38f) was FIXED (nodemailer@^9), NOT allowlisted — if it ever
// regresses, CI must fail. DEP-07 (sharp <0.35 inherited libvips CVEs,
// GHSA-f88m-g3jw-g9cj, high) was likewise FIXED, NOT allowlisted: sharp bumped to
// ^0.35.3 in backend + frontend, with a root `overrides.sharp` so next's optional copy
// collapses to the patched version too. sharp decodes untrusted child-uploaded evidence
// photos (backend/src/services/storage.ts) — runtime-reachable, so it must be patched,
// never ignored. DEP-06 (GHSA-8988, otel baggage) is a RUNTIME advisory
// that IS allowlisted as a time-boxed, owner-approved risk acceptance because no
// compatible fix exists yet (details by its allowlist entry below).
//
// RE-TRIAGE: remove an entry here when a non-downgrade patch lands upstream, or
// when an advisory escalates to critical / moves into the runtime path. Keep
// this file and security_assessment.md (§ Dependency Audit) in sync.
{
  // Fail on moderate, high, and critical advisories (anything not allowlisted).
  "moderate": true,

  "allowlist": [
    // DEP-02 — serialize-javascript RCE, via workbox-build -> next-pwa@5 (PWA BUILD only).
    "GHSA-5c6j-r48x-rmvq",

    // DEP-03 (postcss XSS, GHSA-qx2v-qp2m-jg93) REMOVED 2026-07-23 — FIXED, not allowlisted.
    // postcss bumped to ^8.5.22 in frontend + a root `overrides.postcss` so next's vendored copy
    // collapses to the patched version too. The bump was forced by GHSA-6g55-p6wh-862q (high,
    // CVSS 7.5: arbitrary file read / info disclosure via an attacker-controlled sourceMappingURL
    // in a CSS comment, range <=8.5.11), published after this repo's last green run on main.
    // npm's own "fix" for it was next@9.3.3 — a multi-major DOWNGRADE — so the override is the
    // correct route. 8.5.22 clears both advisories, which is why the old XSS entry goes with it:
    // per the note above, a dead allowlist entry is not harmless.

    // DEP-04 — minimatch ReDoS x3, via @typescript-eslint -> eslint-config-next@14 (LINT only).
    "GHSA-3ppc-4f35-3m26",
    "GHSA-7r86-cg39-jmmj",
    "GHSA-23c5-xmqv-rm74",

    // DEP-05 (js-yaml DoS, GHSA-h67p-54hq-rp68) REMOVED 2026-07-21 — the advisory no longer
    // appears in `npm audit`, so the entry was dead. A dead entry is not harmless: it would
    // silently suppress the same advisory if a future dependency reintroduced it.

    // DEP-06 — @opentelemetry/core: unbounded memory allocation in W3C Baggage propagation
    // (moderate, CVSS 5.3, CWE-770). RUNTIME advisory, transitive via
    // @sentry/node@9.47.1 -> bundled @opentelemetry/* -> @opentelemetry/core@1.30.1 (A4).
    // NO COMPATIBLE FIX: the patch is only in @opentelemetry/core >= 2.8.0 (a major bump);
    // Sentry 9.47.1 pins otel core ^1.30.1 across its 1.x SDK packages, and there is no 1.x
    // backport, so forcing 2.8.0 would break Sentry's runtime.
    // ACCEPTED RISK (owner-approved 2026-07-01), mitigated by: Node's default 16KB
    // max-http-header-size bounding the `baggage` header, Cloudflare in front, global rate
    // limiting, and tracesSampleRate=0. RE-TRIAGE: remove when @sentry/node depends on
    // @opentelemetry/core >= 2.8.0. See security_assessment.md § Dependency Audit.
    "GHSA-8988-4f7v-96qf",

    // DEP-08 — brace-expansion: DoS via unbounded expansion length causing an OOM process crash
    // (high). Published 2026-07-24/25, AFTER this repo's last green run; it fails on a clean
    // `main` checkout too, so it is unrelated to the FR-18 work that surfaced it.
    // NO FIX EXISTS: the advisory range is `<=5.0.7`, i.e. EVERY published version including the
    // newest. npm's suggested remedy is a semver-major @sentry/node bump to 10.68.0, which would
    // not clear it either — the range covers all versions, so there is nothing to upgrade to.
    // Mostly dev/build-only (eslint, jest, next-pwa's workbox build, rimraf, ts-jest), BUT it is
    // also reachable in a PRODUCTION install:
    //   @sentry/node@9.47.1 -> minimatch@9.0.9 -> brace-expansion@2.1.2
    // so it is recorded as runtime-reachable rather than dismissed as dev-only.
    // ACCEPTED RISK, low exploitability: the DoS requires an attacker-controlled brace/glob
    // pattern. Nothing in backend/src, frontend/src or shared/src uses minimatch, glob or
    // braceExpand directly (verified by grep), and no Sentry pattern options
    // (ignoreTransactions / denyUrls / allowUrls / tracePropagationTargets) are configured, so
    // the only patterns ever expanded are the SDK's own constants. No request data reaches it.
    // RE-TRIAGE: remove as soon as a patched brace-expansion ships and the dependency tree can
    // reach it — this entry exists only because there is currently nothing to upgrade to.
    "GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg"
  ]
}
